Please use the information below to generate a latex article for my website. I want to explore constraint torques and forces that may be present in the swing and show how they can be used for force and torque transmission. The body part I would like to focus on first is the left wrist joint, which can be seen as universal joint.

Here's a hypothesis - the angle at which you grip the club relative to the axis of the wrist joint may influence variability of the face angle. Its a long drawn out one but I think there's some really interesting payoff. It may also be a part of the reason why we rarely play with the club and arms on the same plane - it could be a mechanical disadvantage.

I have modeled the wrists as a universal joint at the wrist. Essentially flexion / extension is one axis and radial / ulnar is the other axis. The forearm can rotate around its axis. I can apply a torque on either of these axes. We have muscles that allow us to basically do these two motions on these two axis.

If I drive the model with forward dynamics and apply torque to the X and Y axis, I can then measure the torques seen at the joint. (This is just an option in the Matlab joint block - you specify input torques and sense output torques). When I look at the sensed values, there are not 2 torques (X,Y) but three (X,Y,Z). Furthermore, the X and Y values are not necessarily the same as the input torques used to drive the joint. There are passive components of the torque that are seen at the wrist due to the interaction of either the momentum of the system interacting with constraints, or applied torques interacting with constraints. (The original goal was to drive my model kinematically and then grab the kinetics from this and input them into the kinetically driven model but this realization threw a roadblock in the way that has turned into a nice detour).

Additionally, if you take a driven universal joint like the wrist and apply torques in two directions, you can actually induce a torque on the third axis. You can try this by firmly holding your left wrist with your right hand and preventing all forearm rotation. Now simultaneously torque towards flexion and ulnar with the left wrist. You can feel the left wrist torquing against the resistance of the right hand.

Universal joints are used to transmit force on the z axis of the two connected segments. When you apply a torque about the z axis of one segment, it transmits this torque to the z axis of the connected segment. It does this not actively but via what are known as constraint torques. These are torques that exist as the system pushes in directions in which it does not have degrees of freedom to move. They don't do any work, but they are really good at transmitting forces and torques from one place to another (which might be quite handy in say a golf swing where you would want to create a torque with large muscles and then transfer this out to the hands and club). This robotics video has some info on what they are ([https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/nu-gm-book-resource/8-7-constrained-dynamics/](https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/nu-gm-book-resource/8-7-constrained-dynamics/))

Due to their nature, constraint torques are likely quite variable. They are nonlinear, unpredictable, and dependent on may different things - momentum, system configuration, applied torques, etc. 

Now for the angle bit I originally mentioned - if we lined the "hand side" of the wrist universal joint up at an axis perpendicular to the shaft (think gripping deep down towards the fingers) then any of the torques transmitted via constraint torques would be transmitted along the local alpha axis of the shaft - the in swing plane motion (at least near impact). There is a lot of inertia on the club in this axis and the angular acceleration from any applied torque will be lower.  This would help transmit torque through the wrists to the club in a way that would produce translation of the clubhead which is likely good for generating CHS.

In contrast if we let the butt of the club get up into the palm, the z axis of the lower portion of the universal joint would be more in line with the shaft. This would result in constraint torques from the wrist being transmitted to more about the shaft rotation. The constraint torques being transmitted by the wrist would now be directed into an axis with lower inertia and one in which rotation has a larger effect on the position of the clubface. This seems like a recipe for lower speed and more variable clubface rotation.

